<PageTitle>Authentication basics — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Get started</div>
<h1>Authentication basics</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    A quick mental model for what happens when a user signs in through Vaultex. If you're new to
    OAuth or OpenID Connect, start here before reading the deeper concept guides.
</p>

<h2 id="players">The three players</h2>
<p>Every Vaultex sign-in involves three parties:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The user</strong> &mdash; sitting in front of a browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Your application</strong> &mdash; the thing the user wants to use.</li>
    <li><strong>Vaultex</strong> &mdash; the identity provider that holds the user's credentials.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Your application <em>never sees the user's password</em>. That's the whole point of using an
    identity provider. Vaultex handles authentication and hands your app a signed token that proves
    who the user is.
</p>

<h2 id="flow">What happens when a user signs in</h2>
<ol>
    <li>The user clicks "Sign in" in your app.</li>
    <li>Your app redirects the browser to Vaultex with a request that says <em>"I'm client X, send the user back to URL Y after they sign in."</em></li>
    <li>Vaultex shows the user a sign-in form. They enter their credentials.</li>
    <li>Vaultex redirects the browser back to your app with a one-time <strong>authorization code</strong>.</li>
    <li>Your app's backend exchanges the code for an <strong>access token</strong> and an <strong>ID token</strong>.</li>
    <li>Your app reads the ID token to learn who the user is, and stores a session cookie.</li>
</ol>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--info">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M11 7h2v2h-2zm0 4h2v6h-2zm1-9C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm0 18c-4.41 0-8-3.59-8-8s3.59-8 8-8 8 3.59 8 8-3.59 8-8 8z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">This is the Authorization Code flow</div>
        <p>It's the standard OAuth 2.0 flow for web and native apps. Vaultex requires PKCE on top of it for extra protection &mdash; see <a href="/docs/concepts/oauth-flow">OAuth 2.0 + PKCE</a>.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="tokens">What you get back</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Used for</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>ID token</strong></td><td>Telling your app who the user is. JWT with claims like <code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>.</td><td>~1 hour</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Access token</strong></td><td>Calling protected APIs on behalf of the user.</td><td>~1 hour</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Refresh token</strong></td><td>Getting a new access token without sending the user back through sign-in.</td><td>~14 days</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

